---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import Src from "../components/Src.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";

const bundle = [
  { img: "265", doc: "The wrapper", date: "—",
    what: "«Documenti del Matrimonio tra Vincenzo Falco di Pasquale, e di Chiara Rivetti, ed Andreana Crisci di Arcangelo, e di Sara Ruggiero»",
    plate: "processetto-1814-cover.jpg" },
  { img: "266", doc: "Vincenzo Falco's baptism extract", date: "issued 13 Sept 1814",
    what: "Copied from liber IX, fol. 47 a tergo. Baptised 1 February 1792, named Vincentius Raphael.",
    plate: "vincenzo-falco-baptism-1792.jpg" },
  { img: "268", doc: "Adriana Crisci's baptism extract", date: "issued 13 Sept 1814",
    what: "Liber IX, fol. 94 a tergo. Baptised 30 November 1796, daughter of Archangelo Crisci and Sara Ruggiero.",
    plate: "adriana-crisci-baptism-1796.jpg" },
  { img: "269", doc: "Notarial act 85 — the groom's parents consent", date: "8 December 1814",
    what: "Before Felice Morgillo. «li conjugi PASQUALE FALCO del fu MATTEO, e CHIARA RIVETTI del fu NICOLA…»",
    plate: "falco-consent-1814-act85.jpg" },
  { img: "270", doc: "Notarial act 26 — the bride's parents consent", date: "8 December 1814",
    what: "Before Salvio Morgillo. «li conjugi ARCANGELO CRISCI fu NICOLA, e SARA RUGGIERO di Pasquale…»",
    plate: "crisci-consent-1814-act26.jpg" },
  { img: "271", doc: "Civil publication act 33 — first banns", date: "Sunday 11 September 1814",
    what: "Read by the sindaco before the door of the town hall at one o'clock. States four ages.",
    plate: "falco-crisci-banns-1814.jpg" },
  { img: "272", doc: "Civil publication act 40 — second banns", date: "Sunday 18 September 1814",
    what: "The same four ages, repeated a week later — an independent check on the reading.", plate: "" },
  { img: "273", doc: "The archpriest's certificate of the church banns", date: "Sunday 9 October 1814",
    what: "Giacinto Morgillo certifies the denuncia was read in Sant'Andrea and no impediment was raised.", plate: "" },
];

const ages = [
  { who: "Vincenzo Falco", said: "23", implies: "c. 1791", against: "baptised 1 Feb 1792 — he was 22 years 7 months", ok: true },
  { who: "Andreana Crisci", said: "17 compiti", implies: "c. 1797", against: "baptised 30 Nov 1796 — days off her eighteenth", ok: true },
  { who: "Chiara Rivetti", said: "47", implies: "c. 1767", against: "her 1843 burial implied 1763; the tree says 1772", ok: null },
  { who: "Pasquale Falco", said: "60", implies: "c. 1754", against: "baptised 24 Oct 1767 — he was 46. Wrong by fourteen.", ok: false },
];

const series = [
  ["Banns, 11 Sept 1814", "23", "22y 7m", "right — in his 23rd year", true],
  ["Banns, 18 Sept 1814", "23", "22y 7m", "right", true],
  ["Marriage act, 15 Dec 1814", "21", "22y 10m", "wrong by 2", false],
  ["Raffaele I's birth, 25 Apr 1816", "35", "24", "wrong by 11", false],
  ["Raffaele II's birth, 10 May 1818", "27", "26y 3m", "right — in his 27th year", true],
  ["A declaration, 1831", "38", "39", "close", null],
  ["Death register, 1839", "42", "47", "wrong by 5", false],
];
---
<Base title="The Processetti"
      eyebrow="The marriage files — 321 volumes, one read"
      dek="A processetto is everything a couple had to prove before they could marry: their baptisms, their parents' consent, their banns. The first one this archive opened turned out to be the richest document it has.">

  <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1.2rem">
    <p><strong>Under the Napoleonic code a marriage generated a file, not just an entry.</strong>
    The couple had to produce baptism extracts, death certificates for any dead parent, the consent
    of living parents where either was under age, and proof that the banns had been read twice in
    the town and once in the church. All of it was bundled and kept. The bundles survive for the
    Valle Caudina as <strong>321 volumes</strong> — and until 13 September 2026 this archive had
    never read one.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Bundle 16 of 1814 — Falco × Crisci</h2>
  <p class="lede">The file behind <P n="Vincenzo Falco" bold={true} />'s marriage to
  <P n="Andreana Crisci" bold={true} /> on 15 December 1814. Eight documents, three hands, four
  months. <Src ark="an_ua14154" img={265} note="Arienzo processetti 1814" /></p>

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Image</th><th>Document</th><th>Date</th><th>What it says</th><th>Plate</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {bundle.map((b) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">{b.img}</td>
          <td><strong>{b.doc}</strong></td>
          <td class="mono small">{b.date}</td>
          <td class="small">{b.what}</td>
          <td>{b.plate ? <Src plate={b.plate} label="plate" /> : <span class="small" style="opacity:.5">—</span>}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <p class="small">Every weekday the documents claim — 11 September, 18 September and 9 October 1814,
  each called <em>giorno di Domenica</em> — is in fact a Sunday. The bundle checks out against the
  calendar.</p>

  <h2>What it settled</h2>

  <div class="card" style="margin:1.1rem 0">
    <p><strong>Matteo Falco, named in 1814.</strong> Vincenzo was twenty-three — under the Code's
    threshold of twenty-five — so his parents had to consent in person. They did, before the notary
    Felice Morgillo: «li conjugi <strong>PASQUALE FALCO del fu MATTEO</strong>, e <strong>CHIARA
    RIVETTI del fu NICOLA</strong>, domiciliati … <strong>strada Camellara</strong>».
    This is <strong>the earliest document that names <P n="Matteo Falco" bold={true} /></strong>, and
    the earliest that proves him dead. His death bracket closes from <span class="mono">[1786, Oct
    1822]</span> to <span class="mono">[1786, 8 Dec 1814]</span>.
    <Src plate="falco-consent-1814-act85.jpg" label="the act" /></p>
  </div>

  <h3>Vincenzo Falco's baptism — and the end of the age problem</h3>
  <p>The archpriest <strong>Giacinto Morgillo</strong> copied it out of the parish register on
  13 September 1814: «Anno Domini … <strong>1792</strong>, die vero prima <strong>1 Februarii</strong>
  … baptizavi infantem natum ex <strong>Paschale Falco</strong>, et <strong>Clara Rivetta</strong>
  Conjugibus … cui dedi nomen <strong>Vincentius Raphael</strong>.»
  <Src plate="vincenzo-falco-baptism-1792.jpg" label="the extract" /></p>

  <p>This archive has published his stated ages as a warning for a long time — twenty-one, then
  thirty-five, then twenty-seven, then forty-two. Measured against a real birth date, <strong>three
  of them are exactly right</strong>, provided you count the way the clerks counted: by the year a
  man is <em>in</em>, not the years he has finished.</p>

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Stated</th><th>Actually</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {series.map(([rec, said, real, v, ok]) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{rec}</td>
          <td class="mono"><strong>{said}</strong></td>
          <td class="mono">{real}</td>
          <td class="small" style={ok === true ? "color:var(--good,inherit)" : ok === false ? "opacity:.75" : "opacity:.6"}>
            {ok === true ? "✓ " : ""}{v}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <p class="small">He was also christened <strong>Vincenzo Raffaele</strong> — which is why he named
  a son Raffaele in 1816 and, when that child died, named the next one Raffaele too. He was passing
  on his own second name, not a grandfather's.</p>

  <h3>Adriana Crisci — and a conflict closed</h3>
  <p>The same archpriest, the same day: «… <strong>1796</strong>, die vero trigesima
  <strong>30 Novembris</strong> … infantem natam ex <strong>Archangelo Crisci</strong>, et
  <strong>Sara Ruggiero</strong> Conjugibus … cui dedit nomen <strong>Adriana</strong>.»
  <Src plate="adriana-crisci-baptism-1796.jpg" label="the extract" /></p>
  <p><strong>Adriana is the name she was given at the font</strong> — and the root of every variant
  the registers throw at her: Andreana, Andriana, Andrea. The 1839 death register was read here as
  «Andriana <strong>Cioffi</strong>», and this site carried Crisci-versus-Cioffi as a live conflict.
  Six documents of 1814 say <strong>Crisci</strong> and four of them name her parents. The conflict
  is closed, and the wrong reading stays on <a href={u("/corrections")}>Corrections</a> rather than
  being quietly swapped.</p>

  <h3>Two new generations</h3>
  <p>The bride's consent act names people no record had reached before —
  <strong>NICOLA CRISCI</strong>, Arcangelo's father, already dead by December 1814
  (<em>fu</em>), and <strong>PASQUALE RUGGIERO</strong>, Sara's father, still living
  (<em>di</em>, not <em>fu</em>). And it puts the Crisci on <strong>strada Camellara</strong>:
  the two families were neighbours before they were in-laws.
  <Src plate="crisci-consent-1814-act26.jpg" label="the act" /></p>

  <h2>The four ages in one act</h2>
  <p class="lede">The banns state everyone's age at once — which makes them a controlled experiment
  in how much a register clerk actually knew.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Said, Sept 1814</th><th>Implies born</th><th>Against the record</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {ages.map((a) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{a.who}</strong></td>
          <td class="mono">{a.said}</td>
          <td class="mono small">{a.implies}</td>
          <td class="small">{a.ok === true ? "✓ " : a.ok === false ? "✗ " : ""}{a.against}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="card" style="margin:1.1rem 0">
    <p>The clerk got <strong>both young people right to the year</strong> and <strong>the father
    wrong by fourteen</strong>. That is the thesis this archive has been arguing across a dozen
    volumes — the registers are accurate about whoever is standing in front of the clerk and careless
    about everyone else — and here a single document proves it in both directions at once.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>A standing question, half of it now closed</h2>
  <p><P n="Vincenzo Falco" bold={true} />'s sister <P n="Anna Falco" bold={true} /> (d. 23 August 1820,
  aged 25) was the wife of an <strong>Arcangelo Crisci</strong>. Andreana's father is an
  <strong>Arcangelo Crisci</strong>, of the same street. This site has asked before whether that is
  one man twice married or two men of a name — and the 1817 birth register already answered half of
  it: <strong>two Arcangelo Crisci declare births in that single year, one aged 52 and one aged
  30</strong>.</p>

  <div class="card" style="margin:1.1rem 0">
    <p><strong>The baptism decides which one fathered Andreana, by arithmetic.</strong> She was
    baptised on 30 November 1796, so her father was a married man with a child that year. The elder
    Arcangelo — 52 in 1817, so born about 1765 — was <strong>31</strong> in 1796. The younger — 30 in
    1817, born about 1787 — was <strong>nine years old</strong>. He cannot be her father.</p>
    <p class="small">So <strong>Andreana Crisci's father is the elder Arcangelo Crisci, born about
    1765</strong>. That is fixed.</p>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Which of them Anna Falco married is still not fixed.</strong> This archive's standing
  reading is the younger, and it remains the simpler one — Anna was born about 1795 and married by
  about 1819. But the elder cannot be ruled out: it would need Sara Ruggiero to have died between
  December 1814, when she stood before a notary and consented to her daughter's marriage, and about
  1818. <strong>The test is Sara Ruggiero's death</strong>, and this dossier has turned that from a
  vague question into a dated, falsifiable search — the Arienzo death registers, 1815 to 1818.
  See <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open Questions</a>.</p>

  <h2>How this file was nearly lost</h2>
  <div class="card" style="margin:1.1rem 0;border-left:3px solid var(--rule,currentColor)">
    <p>An earlier session worked this volume from the back, found a bundle numbered 25 and then the
    1815 divider, and concluded that the volume stopped short of act 26. It wrote the conclusion into
    the research queue under <em>“Dead ends, recorded so they are not repeated — do not search these
    two again.”</em></p>
    <p><strong>It was wrong.</strong> Processetti bundles are numbered <strong>in the order the banns
    were published</strong>, not by the number the marriage later received in the marriage register.
    This couple published in September and married in December: <strong>bundle 16, act 26</strong>.
    Bundle 25's publication is dated 20 November — two months <em>later</em> than theirs. The volume
    was never truncated; the search was looking for the wrong number.</p>
    <p class="small">Two rules came out of it. <strong>In a processetti volume, match on the names and
    the publication date, never on the marriage act number.</strong> And a wrong negative is the most
    expensive mistake an archive can make, because it stops anyone looking again — so every
    “do not search this again” note here now carries the reasoning that produced it, where the
    reasoning can be checked.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What remains</h2>
  <p><strong>320 volumes.</strong> Arienzo's own runs are the priority; <strong>Moiano (99
  volumes)</strong> and <strong>Paolisi (43)</strong> have never been opened. Each bundle carries
  baptism extracts, which reach a generation earlier than the civil registers begin — this one
  reached back to a parish register of 1792 that is not itself online. That is the argument for
  reading the rest. <a href={u("/antenati")}>How these are read →</a></p>

</Base>
